  "examples": [
   {
    "title": "Electric iron: running and starting watts are equal",
    "text": "Running 1,000 W, starting 1,000 W. With no start surge the running watts × 1.25 = 1,250 W decide the generator: 1.56 kVA, so the smallest common size, 2.5 kVA."
   },
   {
    "title": "Inverter fridge: a small start",
    "text": "Running 120 W, starting 200 W, against about 1,200 W for a non-inverter fridge; the generator must deliver 200 W."
   },
   {
    "title": "1 ton non-inverter AC: the start decides",
    "text": "Running 1,200 W, starting 3,600 W. The start is far above 1,200 W × 1.25, so the generator must deliver 3,600 W, 4.5 kVA: a 5 kVA set."
   },
   {
    "title": "1 ton inverter AC: a small peak still decides",
    "text": "Running 900 W, highest draw 1,200 W. Running × 1.25 = 1,125 W is below that peak, so the peak of 1,200 W sets the size: 1.5 kVA and a 2.5 kVA set, against 5 kVA for the non-inverter AC."
   },
   {
    "title": "Fridge and iron together",
    "text": "Running watts add up: 1,200 W. Starting watts add only the fridge's jump on top: 2,200 W, not the two starting figures added. The generator must deliver 2,200 W, a 3 kVA set."
   }
  ],
  "table": {
   "caption": "Running vs starting watts, the same job done two ways, from the top: AC ceiling fan, DC inverter ceiling fan, inverter fridge, non-inverter fridge, 1 ton inverter AC, 1 ton non-inverter AC, electric kettle (no motor)",
